        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(B\\mid A)>P(B)$, so $A$ attracts $B$.\n\nUse the common intersection $A\\cap B$ to reverse the conditional probability.",
          "worked": "Since $B$ attracts $A$, $P(A\\mid B)>P(A)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(B\\mid A)&=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}\\\\P(B\\mid A)&=\\frac{P(A\\mid B)P(B)}{P(A)}\\\\P(B\\mid A)&>P(B).\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence $A$ attracts $B$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q33-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p35-q33-b-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Show that ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\overline{B}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " repels ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "A",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(A\\mid\\overline{B})<P(A)$, so $\\overline{B}$ repels $A$.\n\nPartition $A$ into the cases $B$ and $\\overline{B}$, then use $P(A\\mid B)>P(A)$.",
          "worked": "Partition $A$ into the disjoint events $A\\cap B$ and $A\\cap\\overline{B}$. Since $B$ attracts $A$, $P(A\\mid B)>P(A)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(A)&=P(A\\mid B)P(B)+P(A\\mid\\overline{B})(1-P(B))\\\\P(A\\mid\\overline{B})&=\\frac{P(A)-P(A\\mid B)P(B)}{1-P(B)}\\\\P(A\\mid\\overline{B})&<\\frac{P(A)-P(A)P(B)}{1-P(B)}\\\\P(A\\mid\\overline{B})&<P(A).\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence $\\overline{B}$ repels $A$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      }
    ]
